  "summary_en": "Mexico's Civil Aviation Law (1995, consolidated through the 14 Nov 2025 reform) regulates civil air transport, airspace use, certification, registration, safety management, liability, insurance and sanctions. Drone content is limited: it defines autonomous, unmanned and remotely piloted aircraft, requires registration, and assigns regulatory authority to the Federal Civil Aviation Agency.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Defines autonomous, unmanned and remotely piloted aircraft (Art. 2)",
   "Federal Civil Aviation Agency issues certification and operating rules for civil and state unmanned aircraft (Art. 6 Bis XIX)",
   "Remotely piloted aircraft systems must be registered; ownership documents are entered in the Aeronautical Registry (Arts. 30, 47)",
   "Fines of 200–5,000 UMA, plus suspension or revocation, for unmanned aircraft owners who breach the law (Art. 88 Bis 1)",
   "Strict liability for surface damage and mandatory insurance for aircraft operators (Arts. 70, 74)"
  ],
